00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:17.600
What is audit logging?

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.630
It's an immutable audit
trail of all the actions

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.240
taken by anyone who interacts
with your data and systems

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:24.660
on GCP.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.150
So you can think of it as a
record of who did what, where,

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.760
when, and why on your systems.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.510
Audit logs are immutable,
and you'll get them for free

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:37.510
for 365 days for your
admin audit logs,

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.880
or for longer if you
want to pay to have

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.940
your ingestion of your
data access audit logs.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.880
So let's look in a bit more
detail at how those work.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.630
Admin activity audit logs
are the most basic audit logs

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.700
that come turned on
for all [? rights ?]

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.690
by default on the vast majority
of all your GCP services.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.360
Like I said, we host
them for free for 3--

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.130
sorry, for 400 days now.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:03.390
And you can see them in your
Stackdriver Logging console.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:07.756
Data access audit logs
need to be turned on.

00:03:07.756 --> 00:03:10.130
You pay for ingestion for each
of these data access audit

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:12.064
logs, but you don't
pay for storage.

00:03:12.064 --> 00:03:13.980
We'll cover how you get
these turned on later.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.660
But what these show
is an additional bit

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:19.440
of information around
who read or wrote data

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.240
from your data sources on GCP.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.280
Finally, we now have
access transparency

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.720
logs as well, which cover
actions by Google Support

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:28.910
and Engineering.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:32.870
So imagine a person created
a bucket on your GCP service

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:35.330
that created an admin
activity audit log.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.910
Then someone uploaded some
data into that bucket,

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:41.060
read it, looked at the data
in the bucket a few times,

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.356
and created a few more
data access audit logs.

00:03:43.356 --> 00:03:45.480
Let's say they were having
a problem with that data

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.690
and they needed to go and
get some help from support.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:50.540
They call up Google Support, and
Google Support goes and looks

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:52.220
at some of the
settings on that bucket

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:55.100
to see if the appropriate
permissions were in place.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.150
I just want to reiterate
Google Support can't

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.240
look at the data that's
inside your bucket,

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:01.040
but we want to log all
of this information

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.100
so that you can get a full
picture of your security

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.184
posture in GCP.

00:04:06.184 --> 00:04:07.600
When the Google
Support technician

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.520
looks at the metadata
and ACLs on the bucket,

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.604
an access transparency
log is created

00:04:11.604 --> 00:04:14.020
that you can also see in your
Stackdriver Logging console.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:20.149
So let's talk about some of
the key features of these.

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:23.044
So like I said, these are
available for nearly every GCP

00:04:23.044 --> 00:04:25.460
service in the case of your
admin activity and data access

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:26.800
audit logs.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.020
Our access transparency logs are
only available on six services.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.059
We just launched them
into beta two weeks ago,

00:04:32.059 --> 00:04:34.100
but we're planning to add
a lot more new services

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:35.790
throughout the
rest of this year.

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.770
They have [? audit ?]
[? grade ?] protections,

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.080
so these logs are immutable.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:41.570
Once you turn them on, you
can't actually delete them.

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:43.520
And the reason for
this is that we

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:45.896
want to make sure that
if a malicious insider

00:04:45.896 --> 00:04:47.270
inside your company
is attempting

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:49.090
to cover their
tracks, these audit

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:50.340
logs can't be deleted by them.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:53.090
So you always have a
record of what's happened.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.290
Retention is 13 months--

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:57.530
400 days-- for admin activity
and access transparency logs.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:02.240
And for data access
audit logs, the billing

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.040
is based on ingestion
and not retention.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.650
So once you turn them on, we'll
charge you to ingest them,

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:09.565
and you can decide whether
to export them or put them

00:05:09.565 --> 00:05:10.190
somewhere else.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:11.856
