just to auditlogsrock,

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:27.720
but let me find out
exactly where that is.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:31.540
So I'm going to look
into here and look

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:36.960
at my service data, policy
delta, my binding deltas.

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:40.260
And I can see the member
who was added here

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:43.376
is auditlogsrock@gmail.com.

00:12:43.376 --> 00:12:48.040
So if I click Show Matching
Entries for just a moment,

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:53.500
I can change this so
that I exclude anything--

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:56.890
so I'm adding my not here--

00:12:56.890 --> 00:13:05.460
that matches user
colon, and then I'm

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:12.301
going to say my
organization is @google.com.

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:19.190
Let me try making sure we still
see the right audit log there.

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:23.030
All right, good, so
that's appropriate.

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:26.580
From here I can, in one
step, just create a metric.

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:31.340
So what this will do is for
any new log entries coming in,

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:36.350
we will check what
logs-based metrics

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:39.170
we actually have, and if it
matches any, will count that.

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:41.990
So we'll basically be
counting any incoming log

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:45.140
entries that match this filter.

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:49.685
So I'm going to name it
LogsFromOutsideMyOrg.

00:13:55.237 --> 00:13:55.820
And that's it.

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:58.022
I'm going to go ahead
and create the metric.

00:13:58.022 --> 00:13:59.480
There are some more
advanced things

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:01.040
you can do with
logs-based metrics,

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:03.860
but we'll save that
for a different day.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:08.360
And you can see this is actually
my second logs-based metric

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:09.690
here.

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:11.820
And now what I want to
do is actually create

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:13.350
an alerting policy from it.

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:17.630
So what I just created
was LogsFromOutsideMyOrg.

00:14:17.630 --> 00:14:21.840
I'm going to go ahead and create
an alert from this metric.

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.810
So this is popping up, the
Stackdriver Monitoring console.

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:31.761
Remember we said there
are two different tools

00:14:31.761 --> 00:14:33.760
within Stackdriver that
we're going to be using.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:35.160
One is Monitoring.

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:38.070
The other is Logging.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:42.480
Also, if you recall, we said
logs-based metrics will only

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:45.190
apply for data going forward.

00:14:45.190 --> 00:14:48.180
So we just created
this logs-based metric.

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:49.560
We won't see any data here.

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:52.490
That's OK.

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:55.710
But I want to say, I want to be
alerted anytime this happens.

00:14:55.710 --> 00:15:02.430
So if I see even one ever, I
want to be alerted for that.

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:04.885
And we'll say
across any resource.

00:15:04.885 --> 00:15:08.290
I'm going to go ahead and
save the alerting condition.

00:15:08.290 --> 00:15:11.880
So this is a Stackdriver
Monitoring alerting UI.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:15.150
The way it works is we're going
to define these conditions.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:16.869
If those conditions
are violated,

00:15:16.869 --> 00:15:18.660
then we'll automatically
create an incident

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:19.920
and send some notifications.

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:23.040
So the next thing we need to
do is choose what notifications

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:24.750
we need to send here.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:28.120
So we have a number of
different notification options--

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:30.630
email, our Cloud
Console mobile app.

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:34.140
Some other commonly used
ones are webhooks, Slack,

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:35.385
and PagerDuty.

00:15:35.385 --> 00:15:38.160
In this case I'll
leave it at email,

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:41.250
and I will send it to
auditlogsrock@gmail.com.

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:46.630
I could add another
notification if I wanted.

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:49.280
I can add some documentation.

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:55.844
Someone added a policy
outside our org.

00:15:55.844 --> 00:15:59.170
Go talk to them.

00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:03.080
I could link to a
playbook here, as well.

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:04.570
And then I can name the policy.

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:12.240
So alert if users
outside my org.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:15.240
And go ahead and save my policy.

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:17.400
All right, so now let's
go ahead and test it.

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:20.850
I'm going to go back to
our IAM page over here.

00:16:25.294 --> 00:16:26.710
And I'm going to
go ahead and test

00:16:26.710 --> 00:16:29.170
this again by adding
another member-- so

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:30.775
auditlogsrock@gmail.com.

00:16:33.532 --> 00:16:34.590
All righty.

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:41.160
And I'll set Random Other
IAM role, and save that.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:43.770
It should take us
just a minute for that

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:45.630
alerting policy to fire.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:48.930
So while we're doing
that, let's go ahead

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:51.780
and talk about another
common use case

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:54.310
that we hear from
customers, which is great,

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:57.990
it's fantastic that we can
alert on our audit logs.

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:01.140
But what I really want
to also be able to do

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:05.369
is to do some automation
based on our audit logs.

00:17:05.369 --> 00:17:08.910
And so here's where the power
of the Stackdriver Logging

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:11.670
platform is really powerful.

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:14.160
So we're going to
start out with--

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:15.720
what we're going to
do is we're going

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:18.000
to take those audit
logs that are coming in.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.069
We're going to export
the matching logs

00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:25.380
that we care about
to a Pub/Sub channel,

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:29.680
and we'll use Cloud Functions
to take action based on that.

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:32.010
So again, the kind
of flow is audit logs

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:33.350
are automatically generated.

00:17:33.350 --> 00:17:34.910
That's fantastic.

00:17:34.910 --> 00:17:36.990
They'll come in to
Stackdriver logging.

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:40.350
From there, they will
go to this log sink

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:41.880
that we need to create.

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:45.210
And then from there
they'll go to Pub/Sub.

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:47.280
We'll use Cloud
Functions to do whatever

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:50.100
kind of crazy
processing we dream up.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:52.410
So for this example,
let's say every time

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:56.709
we start up a new VM we
want to apply a label

00:17:56.709 --> 00:17:58.500
or maybe check to see
if that label exists,

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:01.680
and if it doesn't, then
automatically apply that label.

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:06.030
For example, say that
the environment is prod.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:08.250
So the first thing we
need to go ahead and do

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.450
is create an export.

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:15.600
So you'll see over here, we
started out in the logs viewer.

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:17.100
We tested out logs metrics.

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:21.990
That was how we created
alerting policies.

00:18:21.990 --> 00:18:23.700
From there, we're
going to do exports.

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:25.800
So I'm going to go over
into our Exports tab.

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:29.550
I have a couple of exports
already set up here.

00:18:29.550 --> 00:18:31.530
Similar to how
logs-based metrics work,

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:36.600
exports also work only kind of
going forward for log entries.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:38.400
And so right before
this I did create

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:40.994
the log sink just to make
sure that everything works.

00:18:40.994 --> 00:18:43.410
So I'm going to go ahead and
take a look at that log sink.

00:18:43.410 --> 00:18:46.650
And what I'm saying
is, I want anything--

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:49.230
the colon here does
a substring match,

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:54.510
so anything where the log name
matches my activity logs--

00:18:54.510 --> 00:19:00.900
and anything where the audit
logging protoPayload.methodName

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:01.670
is insert.

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:04.740
And that's the kind of keyword
that you'll see in our docs

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:10.760
is used when a
new VM is created.

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:16.820
And I also want to make sure
that we actually have the type.

00:19:16.820 --> 00:19:21.000
This colon star means
this field exists.

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:22.160
So if I load--

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:24.320
oops, I haven't created
anything recently,

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:27.840
but let me go ahead and
load some older logs here

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:28.550
and take a look.

00:19:28.550 --> 00:19:30.710
And we can see some
examples of this

00:19:30.710 --> 00:19:34.050
as I've spun up, in testing,
some different logs.

00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:37.850
So you can see I created
MyVM1, MyVM2, MyVM3.

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:41.690
Each of these already
happened back in time,

00:19:41.690 --> 00:19:44.030
but if I create the
next one, MyVM4,

00:19:44.030 --> 00:19:48.960
then it should automatically
show up here on the sink.

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:52.260
All right, so I have
my sink working.

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:56.970
From here, I'm actually going
to go to Cloud Functions.

00:19:56.970 --> 00:19:59.610
So there was one other thing
I wanted to call out here

00:19:59.610 --> 00:20:01.780
for just a moment.

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:03.900
The other important
thing about the sink

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:05.480
is where it actually goes.

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:10.290
So if I edit the
sink one last time,

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:12.240
I had to specify the filter.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:14.730
I also had to
specify the location.

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:16.860
So I chose Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:20.280
My options were BigQuery,
Cloud Storage, and Pub/Sub.

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:24.030
This custom destination will
allow you to create and export

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:29.380
to a different location, so for
example to a different project

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:31.630
or even to a different org
if you really wanted to

00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:34.480
and your permissions
were set up correctly.

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:37.210
BigQuery and Pub/Sub
happen in real time.

00:20:37.210 --> 00:20:39.659
Cloud Storage is batched
about once per hour,

00:20:39.659 --> 00:20:41.200
and so it takes a
few hours for those

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:43.564
logs to show up
in that location.

00:20:43.564 --> 00:20:44.980
And we'll talk
about why you might

00:20:44.980 --> 00:20:48.770
want to use these other export
locations in a minute here,

00:20:48.770 --> 00:20:51.250
but we'll focus on Cloud
Pub/Sub for right now.

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:53.920
And as soon as I
chose Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:56.700
I had the option to either
use an existing Pub/Sub

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:59.140
feed, auditLogPubSub,
or to create a new one.

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:02.570
So I'm going to use the one
I already have created here.

00:21:02.570 --> 00:21:05.230
So now I'm going to go
over to my Cloud Functions.

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:11.620
All right, and creating
the Cloud Function

00:21:11.620 --> 00:21:14.320
took a little bit of thought,
so I've done that ahead of time

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:15.000
here.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:17.590
And you can see I've got
this Cloud Function called

00:21:17.590 --> 00:21:19.127
processAuditLogs.

00:21:19.127 --> 00:21:20.710
I'm going to go ahead
and click on it.

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:27.410
I can see in the last hour it
hasn't had to process anything

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:28.520
at all here.

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:31.810
If I zoom out for a few
days, we should probably see,

00:21:31.810 --> 00:21:34.401
yep, I processed a few
things a couple of days ago

00:21:34.401 --> 00:21:36.650
and one not that long ago
just to make sure everything

00:21:36.650 --> 00:21:38.690
was still working.

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:40.110
I can take a look
at the trigger.

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:41.900
So you can set
Cloud Functions up

00:21:41.900 --> 00:21:43.650
to trigger on different things.

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:47.570
In this case, we're triggering
on the auditLogPubSub topic

00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:49.930
that we had specified
in our log sink.

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:51.430
Now let's take a
look at the source.

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:57.430
All right, so what this
Cloud Function is going to do

00:21:57.430 --> 00:22:00.670
is act on a Pub/Sub message.

00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:03.160
We know that the log entries
are what will be coming in

00:22:03.160 --> 00:22:05.630
on that Pub/Sub message.

00:22:05.630 --> 00:22:09.640
So we're going to parse
that Pub/Sub message.

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:12.200
Take a look at the
log entry here.

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:16.390
We have some auth stuff that
I took from our handy examples

00:22:16.390 --> 00:22:18.370
in our documentation.

00:22:18.370 --> 00:22:22.360
And then we're going to check to
make sure that this is actually

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:23.920
a valid log entry for us.

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:27.670
In this case, we only care
about new VMs, so only

00:22:27.670 --> 00:22:29.480
things where it is
actually a log entry

00:22:29.480 --> 00:22:31.750
and there is actually
a request field.

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:35.080
And the method name
of that request field

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:38.950
is compute.instances.insert.

00:22:38.950 --> 00:22:43.030
And then we'll actually pull
the name of the VM out of there.

00:22:43.030 --> 00:22:48.250
And in order to add
a label to that VM,

00:22:48.250 --> 00:22:57.640
we need to first get the labels
that exist on that VM already,

00:22:57.640 --> 00:22:59.950
and then we can add
new ones if needed.

00:22:59.950 --> 00:23:02.270
And so that's what
we're doing here.

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:08.590
We're doing a get on that
new VM, logging things if we

00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:10.390
see an error.

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:12.400
We have to pull out
that fingerprint,

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:14.620
and that's what we
use to actually create

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:20.920
the new label down here
where environment is prod.

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:24.070
And then we'll say,
yay, we set the labels

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:25.900
and exit out of here.

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:37.060
So with that, let's go ahead and
go back to our Compute console.

00:23:37.060 --> 00:23:38.685
And I'm going to
create a new instance.

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:45.156
And I'll call this MyVM4.

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:54.165
Go ahead and set
it as a small one.

00:23:54.165 --> 00:23:56.500
And we'll go ahead
and create it.

00:23:56.500 --> 00:23:59.650
So it'll take just a
moment for that to spin up.

00:23:59.650 --> 00:24:01.790
And I should be able to
see it in my audit log.

00:24:01.790 --> 00:24:05.150
So let's hop over to
the logs viewer here.

00:24:05.150 --> 00:24:08.960
And I'm going to go
back to all my logs.

00:24:08.960 --> 00:24:15.376
And let me switch
to the basic mode.

00:24:15.376 --> 00:24:17.140
All right, and so
now, again, I'm

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:19.630
seeing all my logs
from all of my VMs.

00:24:19.630 --> 00:24:23.870
Instead, I'm just going to
take a look at my audit logs.

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:27.260
And indeed I see that
a new instance, MyVM4,

00:24:27.260 --> 00:24:30.470
was inserted just a moment ago.

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:35.475
So now I can go back
to my Cloud Function.

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:44.481
And we'll take a look
at the Cloud Function

00:24:44.481 --> 00:24:45.480
and what it's processed.

00:24:45.480 --> 00:24:47.730
And you can see, oh yeah,
it did see something

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:49.420
just a moment ago.

00:24:49.420 --> 00:24:53.220
So should really have left the
tab open, but that's all right.

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:55.140
Go back to Compute Engine here.

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:56.304
Take a look at MyVM4.

00:24:56.304 --> 00:24:58.470
And you'll remember I didn't
add any labels whenever

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:01.060
I created this.

00:25:01.060 --> 00:25:05.616
So if I want to make sure
that this worked correctly,

00:25:05.616 --> 00:25:08.330
Show Info Panel.

00:25:08.330 --> 00:25:09.980
And we can see it worked.

00:25:09.980 --> 00:25:13.190
We created a key
with environment

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:15.550
and the value is prod.

00:25:15.550 --> 00:25:19.090
So you can use this for
lots of different things.

00:25:19.090 --> 00:25:23.270
If a firewall rule is
incorrectly configured,

00:25:23.270 --> 00:25:24.550
automatically remove that.

00:25:24.550 --> 00:25:27.200
Or again, if we think
about the example

00:25:27.200 --> 00:25:29.240
we just did with our
alerting, if someone

00:25:29.240 --> 00:25:32.750
adds a user who isn't
part of the organization,

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:35.760
maybe you change the role
that was just created.

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:37.550
The kind of
possibilities here are

00:25:37.550 --> 00:25:39.530
endless because we have
so many different APIs

00:25:39.530 --> 00:25:41.480
that you can use.

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:43.820
So speaking of the thing
that we were alerting on,

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:51.280
let's just hop over to the
email for auditlogsrock,

00:25:51.280 --> 00:25:53.920
and we can see we should have
received an email saying,

00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:55.150
hey, there was an issue.

00:25:55.150 --> 00:25:57.910
And indeed I created a
previous alerting policy.

00:25:57.910 --> 00:26:00.880
So you see it represented
a few times here,

00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:02.740
but I can drill into
this and it does

00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:06.960
let me know that the
alerting policy was created,

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:10.870
that the issue
was detected here.

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:12.150
Great.

00:26:12.150 --> 00:26:19.870
So, moving along then, that was
alerting and creating exports.

00:26:19.870 --> 00:26:22.870
I mentioned there are a few
different types of exports.

00:26:22.870 --> 00:26:25.960
Another type of export that
you might choose to use

00:26:25.960 --> 00:26:28.180
is BigQuery.

00:26:28.180 --> 00:26:32.020
And so I have, if
I go to My Exports,

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:34.810
I can see a number of--

00:26:34.810 --> 00:26:38.800
I've created this thing
for my BigQuery audit logs,

00:26:38.800 --> 00:26:41.980
and I could use BigQuery to
actually explore the logs here,

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:42.490
as well.

00:26:45.076 --> 00:26:46.720
Let's see.

00:26:46.720 --> 00:26:49.360
And then the last thing
that I want to show

00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:52.990
is suppose I wanted to
send all of my old logs

00:26:52.990 --> 00:27:00.850
or all of my audit logs to
GCS for compliance purposes.

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:04.370
I could use a log
sink for that as well.

00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:07.930
So same sort of process
would hold here.

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:12.280
I would choose which
logs I want to export.

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:19.060
And in this case here,
I'll export all of my logs

00:27:19.060 --> 00:27:22.690
from all of my GCE VMs.

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:26.516
And I'll select a
new storage location.

00:27:26.516 --> 00:27:29.850
I'll have to create a
new one, AllGCELogs.

00:27:37.930 --> 00:27:45.040
And I can then go
ahead and create this,

00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:49.369
and that way all my future logs
will be put into that location.

00:27:49.369 --> 00:27:51.160
The thing that we hear
from customers a lot

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:52.600
though is, all
right, that's cool,

00:27:52.600 --> 00:27:55.165
but I have an organization
with thousands of projects.

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:03.040
How do I make sure that I have
log sink set up on all of them?

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:07.970
And so we have a really cool
tool called Aggregated Exports

00:28:07.970 --> 00:28:10.970
which will allow
you to manage that.

00:28:10.970 --> 00:28:13.580
So what Aggregated Exports
will allow you to do--

00:28:13.580 --> 00:28:18.440
and you can do this via the API
or via the gcloud command-line

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:20.030
interface--

00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:23.840
is I can create a log
sink either at the folder

00:28:23.840 --> 00:28:26.420
or at the organization level.

00:28:26.420 --> 00:28:28.430
And if I say
include-children, then it

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:32.180
will not only get the logs
from all of the VMs that I have

00:28:32.180 --> 00:28:34.430
or all of the projects
that I have today

00:28:34.430 --> 00:28:36.920
but also from any new project
that's spun up in the future.

00:28:39.570 --> 00:28:44.890
All right, and with that let's
move on to advanced use cases.

00:28:44.890 --> 00:28:45.820
JOSEPH VALENTE: Cool.

00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:47.980
So Mary's looked
at some of the ways

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:50.722
that we can set up alerting
and exports and storage.

00:28:50.722 --> 00:28:52.180
Let's look at some
of the use cases

00:28:52.180 --> 00:28:55.170
that we might use those in.

00:28:55.170 --> 00:28:57.360
So the first one we have
is insider risk audits.

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:01.004
So we all have sensitive
data in our businesses.

00:29:01.004 --> 00:29:02.670
We all want to make
sure it's protected.

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:05.040
But obviously we need people
to actually go and use

00:29:05.040 --> 00:29:06.207
that data from time to time.

00:29:06.207 --> 00:29:08.498
So what if you want to put
a little bit of an extra bit

00:29:08.498 --> 00:29:10.230
of security on top
of that and audit

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:13.180
some of those accesses that
are particularly sensitive?

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:15.320
So let's say that you
receive in the security team

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:16.970
a tip-off that one
of your employees

00:29:16.970 --> 00:29:18.345
has been looking
up customer data

00:29:18.345 --> 00:29:20.490
without a legitimate
business purpose?

00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:24.060
You might have all of your
storage systems inside GCP.

00:29:24.060 --> 00:29:26.370
You've configured
data-access audit logging,

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:29.080
and it's piping it all
into Stackdriver Logging.

00:29:29.080 --> 00:29:31.018
As Mary showed you,
you can set up exports.

00:29:31.018 --> 00:29:33.017
So you can export them
into an external sim tool

00:29:33.017 --> 00:29:35.980
if that's how you'd
like to view your data,

00:29:35.980 --> 00:29:37.980
or you can even export
them into BigQuery if you

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:40.470
prefer to use BigQuery.

00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:44.370
Once you've done that, you
can search for an actor using

00:29:44.370 --> 00:29:45.680
the principal-email field.

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:48.720
You can audit all the resources
that that person has accessed

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:51.930
in the last seven
days or month, however

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:54.580
long it is you're
auditing their behavior.

00:29:54.580 --> 00:29:56.082
And then in some
cases you can even

00:29:56.082 --> 00:29:57.540
go ahead and audit
the full request

00:29:57.540 --> 00:29:58.650
and respond strings,
which we have

00:29:58.650 --> 00:30:00.160
available in a lot
of our audit logs.

00:30:00.160 --> 00:30:02.535
So you can get a true picture
of exactly what people have

00:30:02.535 --> 00:30:05.810
been doing on your service.

00:30:05.810 --> 00:30:09.184
For compliance, we often
have companies contacting

00:30:09.184 --> 00:30:10.850
us talking about how
they need to retain

00:30:10.850 --> 00:30:14.870
audit logs to comply with
whatever policy or regulation

00:30:14.870 --> 00:30:16.770
that they are subject to.

00:30:16.770 --> 00:30:19.380
For this you might enable
all the audit logs on all

00:30:19.380 --> 00:30:21.055
of your services, for example.

00:30:21.055 --> 00:30:22.680
That would go into
Stackdriver Logging,

00:30:22.680 --> 00:30:24.810
and then you would configure
your export this time

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:26.760
into just the GCS bucket.

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:29.340
And you might configure the
IAM policy on that GCS bucket

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:31.610
to be highly restricted,
so only someone maybe

00:30:31.610 --> 00:30:33.337
in your legal team
can access it.

00:30:33.337 --> 00:30:35.670
And then also you would enable
data-access audit logging

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:37.260
on that bucket
itself to make sure

00:30:37.260 --> 00:30:38.884
that you have an
audit trail of who has

00:30:38.884 --> 00:30:41.167
been accessing the audit logs.

00:30:41.167 --> 00:30:43.250
From that you can generate
reports from your logs,

00:30:43.250 --> 00:30:45.000
turn them over to
auditors, or retain them

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:45.980
for a custom period.

00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:47.480
You can retain them
for however long

00:30:47.480 --> 00:30:48.805
you want in your GCS bucket.

00:30:48.805 --> 00:30:50.180
MARY KOES: One
other cool feature

00:30:50.180 --> 00:30:53.880
that we didn't talk about but
just kind of to call out here

00:30:53.880 --> 00:30:58.020
is we have a feature
called logging exclusions.

00:30:58.020 --> 00:31:03.890
So if you want to store
those logs in a GCS bucket

00:31:03.890 --> 00:31:07.250
but you don't want to pay for
those in Stackdriver Logging--

00:31:07.250 --> 00:31:09.320
specifically for those
data-access logs,

00:31:09.320 --> 00:31:11.420
which can be really
high volume--

00:31:11.420 --> 00:31:15.040
you can use your export
along with a feature called

00:31:15.040 --> 00:31:17.885
exclusions and say,
keep those logs out

00:31:17.885 --> 00:31:19.310
of Stackdriver Logging.

00:31:19.310 --> 00:31:23.490
Because the export happens
before the exclusion,

00:31:23.490 --> 00:31:25.730
those will still be
exported while you

00:31:25.730 --> 00:31:28.430
can choose not to pay for
them in Stackdriver Logging

00:31:28.430 --> 00:31:30.260
if you want those for
compliance purposes

00:31:30.260 --> 00:31:34.850
but don't need them for alerting
or other Stackdriver-related

00:31:34.850 --> 00:31:37.468
functionality,
searching, et cetera.

00:31:37.468 --> 00:31:38.456
Cool.

00:31:38.456 --> 00:31:39.444
JOSEPH VALENTE: Great.

00:31:39.444 --> 00:31:42.880
OK, so stay tuned for some live
Q&amp;A. We'll back in less than a

00:31:42.880 --> 00:31:44.609
minute to answer your questions.

00:33:25.470 --> 00:33:26.640
OK, and we're back.

00:33:26.640 --> 00:33:28.710
Thanks for asking all
these amazing questions.

00:33:28.710 --> 00:33:30.580
We're going to go ahead and
answer a few of them now.

00:33:30.580 --> 00:33:32.830
So the first one is, what
if I want to delete my audit

00:33:32.830 --> 00:33:34.527
logs and not be
charged for them?

00:33:34.527 --> 00:33:35.610
MARY KOES: Great question.

00:33:35.610 --> 00:33:36.526
We get this one a lot.

00:33:36.526 --> 00:33:41.640
So the answer is that
audit logs are charged only

00:33:41.640 --> 00:33:44.325
based on-- all logs are charged
only based on ingestion.

00:33:44.325 --> 00:33:47.400
So you don't need to worry about
managing the volume of data

00:33:47.400 --> 00:33:50.070
that is being stored.

00:33:50.070 --> 00:33:55.590
And also remind folks
that we only charge

00:33:55.590 --> 00:34:01.230
for the data-access logs or
the access-transparency logs,

00:34:01.230 --> 00:34:03.780
not for the
admin-activity logs which

00:34:03.780 --> 00:34:06.510
are available kind
of free of charge

00:34:06.510 --> 00:34:09.662
as part of your use of
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:34:09.662 --> 00:34:11.120
JOSEPH VALENTE:
One other thing I'd

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:14.989
like to add there is just
around deleting projects.

00:34:14.989 --> 00:34:17.239
So as you'd imagine,
when you delete a project

00:34:17.239 --> 00:34:19.850
it will delete the information
around that project.

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:22.791
And that will include the logs
that are inside your project.

00:34:22.791 --> 00:34:25.040
So do make sure that you
have the appropriate controls

00:34:25.040 --> 00:34:27.081
in place to prevent that
from happening if that's

00:34:27.081 --> 00:34:30.179
something you'd like to avoid.

00:34:30.179 --> 00:34:34.670
So the next question is, can
I write my own audit logs?

00:34:34.670 --> 00:34:38.810
MARY KOES: So we do support,
via the logging API,

00:34:38.810 --> 00:34:41.480
writing all sorts
of different logs.

00:34:41.480 --> 00:34:45.650
Audit logs are kind of a special
type of log that we've defined,

00:34:45.650 --> 00:34:49.920
and we write to them on your
behalf as part of Google Cloud

00:34:49.920 --> 00:34:50.630
Platform.

00:34:50.630 --> 00:34:53.239
So you can't write your
own audit logs per se,

00:34:53.239 --> 00:34:55.730
but you can create your own
logs that do anything else

00:34:55.730 --> 00:34:56.900
you want to capture there.

00:34:56.900 --> 00:34:59.820
And we support that via API.

00:34:59.820 --> 00:35:01.850
We also have a logging agent.

00:35:01.850 --> 00:35:04.022
You can send that through
our client library.

00:35:04.022 --> 00:35:05.480
So there are lots
of different ways

00:35:05.480 --> 00:35:10.962
to get your own logs into the
Stackdriver Logging platform.

00:35:10.962 --> 00:35:12.890
JOSEPH VALENTE: Great.

00:35:12.890 --> 00:35:15.200
What actual information
is contained

00:35:15.200 --> 00:35:16.250
inside the audit logs?

00:35:16.250 --> 00:35:18.086
Do you have the location
of the accessor?

00:35:18.086 --> 00:35:19.460
MARY KOES: Want
to take that one?

00:35:19.460 --> 00:35:21.418
JOSEPH VALENTE: This is
a really good question.

00:35:21.418 --> 00:35:23.330
So you can read about
all the information

00:35:23.330 --> 00:35:26.150
that's contained inside the
audit logs on our Google Cloud

00:35:26.150 --> 00:35:29.397
Platform audit-logging
documentation page.

00:35:29.397 --> 00:35:30.980
There's different
types of information

00:35:30.980 --> 00:35:33.600
inside each of the three
different types of logs.

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:36.260
So in your
admin-access API logs,

00:35:36.260 --> 00:35:38.060
there's more information
around people

00:35:38.060 --> 00:35:41.270
who have performed things like,
as Mary was showing you before,

00:35:41.270 --> 00:35:43.802
creation and deletion
of objects and so forth.

00:35:43.802 --> 00:35:45.260
Your data-access
logs, you're going

00:35:45.260 --> 00:35:47.135
to get more information
about a resource that

00:35:47.135 --> 00:35:49.720
was read or written to.

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:53.460
In the access-transparency logs,
you get similar information.

00:35:53.460 --> 00:35:56.330
However, you do get
slightly less information.

00:35:56.330 --> 00:35:59.690
While in your admin activity
and data-access logs,

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:02.250
you will get the principal
email of the actor.

00:36:02.250 --> 00:36:04.070
In your access-transparency
logs you

00:36:04.070 --> 00:36:07.220
won't get the principal
email of the Google employee

00:36:07.220 --> 00:36:08.870
who performed the
access, but you

00:36:08.870 --> 00:36:13.282
will get the location of
where they accessed it from.

00:36:13.282 --> 00:36:15.740
Unfortunately, we don't have
that information available yet

00:36:15.740 --> 00:36:19.760
inside our admin-activity
or data-access logs.

00:36:19.760 --> 00:36:21.500
But if it's of
interest to you, please

00:36:21.500 --> 00:36:22.708
feel free to reach out to us.

00:36:22.708 --> 00:36:24.260
We'd love to hear from you.

00:36:24.260 --> 00:36:30.570
And with respect to
the access-transparency

00:36:30.570 --> 00:36:34.380
logs as well, when we show you
the location of the Googler,

00:36:34.380 --> 00:36:36.420
we also show you
the desk location

00:36:36.420 --> 00:36:38.190
where that Googler is
typically situated.

00:36:38.190 --> 00:36:40.590
So you'll be able to
see that the Googler was

00:36:40.590 --> 00:36:43.680
in the US at the time,
but their desk location

00:36:43.680 --> 00:36:45.270
is typically in Canada.

00:36:45.270 --> 00:36:47.478
That's something that we've
heard from some customers

00:36:47.478 --> 00:36:49.200
as being helpful for
compliance reasons,

00:36:49.200 --> 00:36:52.450
but your mileage may vary.

00:36:52.450 --> 00:36:54.340
MARY KOES: But if we
have any customers who

00:36:54.340 --> 00:36:58.090
are concerned about capturing
the location of the accessor

00:36:58.090 --> 00:37:00.530
for other logs, that generally
isn't something we capture,

00:37:00.530 --> 00:37:01.030
right?

00:37:01.030 --> 00:37:02.464
JOSEPH VALENTE:
That's right, yes.

00:37:02.464 --> 00:37:03.880
MARY KOES: And
last question-- are

00:37:03.880 --> 00:37:07.350
audit logs available
for all our services?

00:37:07.350 --> 00:37:08.550
JOSEPH VALENTE: Tough one.

00:37:08.550 --> 00:37:11.700
They're available for
almost all of our services.

00:37:11.700 --> 00:37:13.650
We're hopefully going
to be able to announce

00:37:13.650 --> 00:37:15.608
later this year that they
are available for all

00:37:15.608 --> 00:37:16.380
of our services.

00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:18.750
Right now you can head to
our Google Cloud Platform

00:37:18.750 --> 00:37:21.690
documentation to see the
full list of services that

00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:24.270
have logs available
for them, and also you

00:37:24.270 --> 00:37:27.510
can read what the state of those
audit-logging integrations are.

00:37:27.510 --> 00:37:29.972
So in some cases
they may be in GA.

00:37:29.972 --> 00:37:32.430
For some other cases we may be
only producing them in beta.

00:37:32.430 --> 00:37:34.510
So you might not be
able to rely on them

00:37:34.510 --> 00:37:37.590
as much as you can for
a GA-style audit log.

00:37:37.590 --> 00:37:39.150
However, you can
be fairly confident

00:37:39.150 --> 00:37:41.149
that, for most services,
by the end of this year

00:37:41.149 --> 00:37:43.020
we should have
audit-logging coverage,

00:37:43.020 --> 00:37:46.440
at least for admin-activity
and data-access audit logging.

00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:50.970
With respect to the
access-transparency logs,

00:37:50.970 --> 00:37:53.370
those are only available for
six services at the moment.

00:37:53.370 --> 00:37:55.980
They're available on Google
Cloud Storage, Google App

00:37:55.980 --> 00:38:02.615
Engine, IAM, Key Management
Service, Persistent Disk, and--

00:38:02.615 --> 00:38:03.520
have I said them all?

00:38:03.520 --> 00:38:04.950
I think I have.

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:06.480
The full list is
available online.

00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:08.880
You can go and head to our
Google Cloud Platform products

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:11.070
page and read on the
access-transparency product

00:38:11.070 --> 00:38:13.380
page which services
they're available for.

00:38:13.380 --> 00:38:15.180
We're constantly
adding new services

00:38:15.180 --> 00:38:18.150
to our access-transparency
integrations.

00:38:18.150 --> 00:38:20.250
We have another four
coming this quarter,

00:38:20.250 --> 00:38:22.470
and we're going to have a
lot more added this year.

00:38:22.470 --> 00:38:25.470
So stay tuned if you're
interested in that.

00:38:25.470 --> 00:38:29.750
You can definitely take
a look at our website.

00:38:29.750 --> 00:38:31.892
And if you want, we can
turn them on for you.

00:38:31.892 --> 00:38:34.350
One thing I will note that I
don't think we've had a chance

00:38:34.350 --> 00:38:38.340
to say yet, though, with respect
to the access-transparency logs

00:38:38.340 --> 00:38:41.160
is that you will need to
have gold or platinum support

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:43.151
enabled to turn those on.

00:38:43.151 --> 00:38:45.150
Once you have gold and
platinum support enabled,

00:38:45.150 --> 00:38:47.134
you can turn
access-transparency logs on,

00:38:47.134 --> 00:38:49.050
and we'll collect and
store those logs for you

00:38:49.050 --> 00:38:50.327
free of charge.

00:38:50.327 --> 00:38:52.410
But unfortunately, we don't
yet offer this service

00:38:52.410 --> 00:38:56.882
to customers who don't have
those support packages enabled.

00:38:56.882 --> 00:38:58.750
MARY KOES: Great, thanks.

00:38:58.750 --> 00:39:00.590
JOSEPH VALENTE: OK,
I think that's all

00:39:00.590 --> 00:39:03.464
we have question-wise.

00:39:03.464 --> 00:39:05.130
As always, you know
where to contact us.

00:39:05.130 --> 00:39:07.950
You can get more support
from Google Support.

00:39:07.950 --> 00:39:09.924
But otherwise, thanks so
much for participating

00:39:09.924 --> 00:39:10.590
in this webinar.

00:39:10.590 --> 00:39:10.760
MARY KOES: Thank you.

00:39:10.760 --> 00:39:13.130
JOSEPH VALENTE: We hope
you've had an amazing journey

00:39:13.130 --> 00:39:15.710
on this audit-logging
journey with us.

00:39:15.710 --> 00:39:18.470
Please tune in next Tuesday for
the next episode of Cloud Live

00:39:18.470 --> 00:39:21.900
OnAir, and thanks very much.

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:23.400
MARY KOES: Bye.

